/-!
# Affine neighbourhoods of finite sets over an affine target

Source: Stacks Project, More on Morphisms, Tag 07S0,
`lemma-separated-locally-quasi-finite-over-affine`
(blueprint: lem-sfb-separated-lqf-affine-finite-set).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Every finite set of points of a scheme separated and locally quasi-finite over an affine
scheme lies in an affine open. Local quasi-finiteness is in the Stacks sense, with its local
finite-type condition. Neither quasi-compactness nor Noetherianity of the source is assumed.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Morphisms, Tag 07S0,
`lemma-separated-locally-quasi-finite-over-affine`
(blueprint: lem-sfb-separated-lqf-affine-finite-set).

Proof:
1. If the finite set is empty, take the empty open; it is affine, being the spectrum of the
   zero ring, or the basic open D(0) in an affine scheme
   (lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-section-vanishing-prime).
2. Otherwise choose an affine neighbourhood of each of its finitely many points
   (lem-weil-pbc-affine-opens-basis). Their finite union U is quasi-compact since affine schemes
   are quasi-compact (lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs).
3. Restrict f to U. It remains locally quasi-finite
   (lem-weil-pbc-lqf-restriction-to-opens). Its diagonal is the base change of the diagonal of f,
   since the open inclusion is mono (def-pc-fibre-product, def-weil-open-immersion). Thus it
   remains separated (lem-weil-pbc-closed-immersion-base-change).
4. The affine target is quasi-separated (lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs), so U to Y is
   quasi-compact (lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence), and it is locally of finite type
   (lem-weil-pbc-lqf-locally-finite-type). Zariski's Main Theorem makes it quasi-affine
   (lemma-nagata-quasi-affine-morphism-of-quasi-finite). Since Y is affine, U identifies with
   an open O in an affine spectrum (def-nagata-quasi-affine-morphism).
5. Choose principal opens D(a_i) inside O around the finitely many image primes
   (lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open). The ideal J generated by the a_i is
   contained in none of these primes. Prime avoidance gives an element a of J in none of them
   (lem-weil-pbc-prime-avoidance). Then D(a) contains the image set and lies in O: every prime
   outside O contains every a_i and hence a.
6. The principal open D(a) is affine (lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-section-vanishing-prime).
   Pull it back along U isomorphic to O. This gives an affine open W of U, hence of X,
   containing the original finite set (def-weil-open-immersion). -/
theorem exists_isAffineOpen_of_finite_of_isLocallyQuasiFinite
    {X Y : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ Y) [IsAffine Y] [IsSeparated f]
    (hf : f.IsLocallyQuasiFinite) (E : Set X) (hE : E.Finite) :
    ∃ W : X.Opens, IsAffineOpen W ∧ E ⊆ W := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
